      "title": "START HERE",
      "properties": {},
      "widgets_values": [
        "# Compose Layers to PSD — build a layered file out of ComfyUI\n\nThree different images go in; one **layered .psd** comes out, written into\n`ComfyUI/input/` as `compose_00001.psd`. Open that file in Photoshop and all\nthree are separate, editable layers.\n\n**This demo runs with no Photoshop at all.** `mode` is set to **Don't open\n(composite only)**, so the node writes the file and hands the result straight\nback — nothing launches, nothing waits, no model is loaded. Queue it and watch.\n\n**Try it:**\n\n1. Queue it as-is and look at the three previews on the right.\n2. Open `ComfyUI/input/compose_00001.psd` in Photoshop and check the Layers\n   panel — three layers, named.\n3. Now switch `mode` to **Wait for first save** and queue again. This time it\n   opens the PSD for you and the run waits until you save.\n\n`example.png` is the image ComfyUI ships with. Swap it for your own — any size\nworks, see the note about the canvas.\n"
      ],
      "color": "#432",
      "bgcolor": "#653"
    },
    {
      "id": 10,
      "type": "MarkdownNote",
      "pos": [
        640,
        -300
      ],
      "size": [
        520,
        660
      ],
      "flags": {},
      "order": 9,
      "mode": 0,
      "inputs": [],
      "outputs": [],
      "title": "Layer order, size and names",
      "properties": {},
      "widgets_values": [
        "# The layer stack\n\n`image_1` is the **bottom** layer, `image_2` sits on top of it, and so on up.\nConnect as many as you want — the node always keeps one empty socket waiting\nand grows to twenty.\n\n**The canvas is sized to the biggest input.** Here the blue backdrop is\n1024×1024, the photo is 768×768, and the inverted copy is 384×384 — so the PSD\nis 1024×1024 and the two smaller ones land **centred**. Nothing is ever scaled\nto fit. Change the numbers on **Empty Image** and you'll see the canvas follow.\n\n**Name your layers:** double-click an `image_N` socket and type a name. That\nname becomes the layer's name in the PSD. The three sockets here are already\nnamed *Backdrop*, *Photo* and *Inverted inset* — open the file in Photoshop and\nyou'll see exactly those in the Layers panel. Rename one and queue again.\n\n**One growing review document:** the **Browse…** button on the node points\n`existing_psd_path` at a PSD you already have. Every run then appends a new\ngroup to that one file instead of writing a fresh file each time.\n"
      ],
      "color": "#432",
      "bgcolor": "#653"
    },
    {
      "id": 11,
      "type": "MarkdownNote",
      "pos": [
        1200,
        -300
      ],
      "size": [
        520,
        660
      ],
      "flags": {},
      "order": 10,
      "mode": 0,
      "inputs": [],
      "outputs": [],
      "title": "What comes back out",
      "properties": {},
      "widgets_values": [
        "# The four outputs\n\n**image** — the flattened composite: exactly what the PSD looks like with every\nlayer visible. Top preview.\n\n**mask** — marks any part of the canvas that no layer covers. All black in this\ndemo, because the backdrop covers the whole thing. Shrink the backdrop and\nyou'll see it light up.\n\n**layers** — one image per layer, each sitting where it really lands on the\nshared canvas. That's the middle preview: three frames you can step through.\nThe `image` output only ever shows the flattened version, so this is how you\nlook at layers individually.\n\n**filename** — the name of the file that was written, relative to\n`ComfyUI/input/`. Shown as text at the bottom. Copy that name into a **Load\nPSD** node's `psd` widget and you've chained this demo into\n`load-psd-start-a-workflow.json`. You do that by hand on purpose — `psd` is a\ndropdown, so there's no socket to run a wire into.\n"
      ],
      "color": "#432",
      "bgcolor": "#653"
    }
